ERROR REPORTS

Please send errors and suggestions to Frans Kaashoek and Robert Morris
(kaashoek,rtm@mit.edu).  The main purpose of xv6 is as a teaching
operating system for MIT's 6.1810, so we are more interested in
simplifications and clarifications than new features.

BUILDING AND RUNNING XV6

You will need a RISC-V "newlib" tool chain from
https://github.com/riscv/riscv-gnu-toolchain, and qemu compiled for
riscv64-softmmu.  Once they are installed, and in your shell
search path, you can run "make qemu".
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
xv6 Process Manager
Extensions to xv6-riscv, MIT's teaching operating system, adding process monitoring and scheduling features.

Author: Marryam Hijab Course: Operating System

MODULES ADDED FOR EXTENSION:
1. Process Monitoring: ps lists running processes with PID, state, memory and priority 
2. Syscall Tracing: trace prints the system calls a program makes 
3. System Information: sysinfo shows free memory and number of processes 
4. Priority Scheduler: nice sets process priority so important processes get more CPU 
5. Lazy Memory Manager: memory is allocated only when a program actually uses it
6. Kernel Log: kernel events (process created, process exited, page fault) are saved in a buffer and can be read with dmesg
7. Uptime: shows how long the system has been running
8. Syscall Statistics: shows how many times each system call has been used
9. Process Killer: terminates processes by name instead of PID
10. CPU Usage: tracks CPU time used by each process and shows it in ps
